WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.900
Who owns it?

2
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.472
The most expensive mistakes in
this book are ownership gaps:

3
00:00:04.472 --> 00:00:07.472
a circuit bought before
anyone owned the governor,

4
00:00:07.472 --> 00:00:10.472
a cutover before anyone
filed the boundary change,

5
00:00:10.472 --> 00:00:14.687
a telemetry contract cancelled before
its replacement was confirmed.

6
00:00:14.687 --> 00:00:17.076
So the rule is one decider per row.

7
00:00:17.076 --> 00:00:22.375
The sponsor and CIO decide the governor
platforms and the order of branch waves.

8
00:00:22.375 --> 00:00:26.618
The authorizing official
decides the boundary change,

9
00:00:26.618 --> 00:00:30.400
the SC-7(4) exception and
every standing exception.

10
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.788
The CISO sets the control requirements
and owns confirming CISA telemetry parity.

11
00:00:35.788 --> 00:00:38.286
The system owner decides, site by site,

12
00:00:38.286 --> 00:00:42.476
whether the governor is live and
logging before traffic moves.

13
00:00:42.476 --> 00:00:45.863
Security architecture
decides TIC conformance

14
00:00:45.863 --> 00:00:49.250
and writes target attributes
into every refresh.

15
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:53.970
And under the Federal Acquisition
Regulation only a contracting officer

16
00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:58.613
can enter into or terminate a contract,
so no engineer can cancel a circuit.

17
00:00:58.613 --> 00:01:02.380
Network engineering and security
operations do the work.
